{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TbxM9WZEFgTH1dL2sNNX3z","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NIST CODATA lattice parameter of silicon 5.431020511e-10 m\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787\"},{\"title\":\"A vacuum double-crystal spectrometer for reference-free highly charged ions X-ray spectroscopy\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.4520\"},{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2010\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.5425\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2002\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2002.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 1998\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_1998.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/691991\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/1307901\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856\"}]\n\nThe value you searched for matches the 2018 CODATA adjustment. NIST's 2018 X-ray values table lists the lattice parameter of silicon (in vacuum, 22.5 °C) as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ lattice parameter∗ of Si (in vacuum, 22.5 ◦C) a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m 1.6× 10−8. The same table gives the {220} lattice spacing as d₂₂₀ = 1.920 155 716(32) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with the same relative uncertainty.\n\nThe table defines this quantity as the unit-cell edge length of an ideal, impurity-free, nearly perfect single crystal of natural silicon. Silicon is a cubic crystal with n = 8 atoms per facecentered cubic unit cell, so d₂₂₀ is the lattice parameter divided by √8, which the CODATA 2010 report also describes.\n\nEarlier adjustments give different numbers. The 2002 table lists d₂₂₀ as 192.015 5965(70) × 10⁻¹² m, and the 1998 table lists 192.015 5845(56) × 10⁻¹² m ({220} lattice spacing of Sia/ √8 d220 192.015 5965(70)× 10−12 m 3.6× 10−8 and {220} lattice spacing of Sia/ √8 d220 192.015 5845(56)× 10−12 m 2.9× 10−8). The search did not return the 2022 CODATA lattice parameter value, so I can't confirm whether it has changed since 2018.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ada1e56-dfe9-49b0-8def-90a47f44fa87","uuid":"ee03a1d2-8a17-4176-935d-14ae181f2cc9","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:48:48.046Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"NIST CODATA lattice parameter of silicon 5.431020511e-10 m","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01TaGkkWbK7hFwHRzQyZZRSw","content":[{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787"},{"title":"A vacuum double-crystal spectrometer for reference-free highly charged ions X-ray spectroscopy","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.4520"},{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2010","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.5425"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2002","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2002.pdf"},{"title":"xray 1998","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_1998.pdf"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/691991"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/1307901"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856"}]},"The value you searched for matches the 2018 CODATA adjustment. NIST's 2018 X-ray values table lists the lattice parameter of silicon (in vacuum, 22.5 °C) as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ lattice parameter∗ of Si (in vacuum, 22.5 ◦C) a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m 1.6× 10−8. The same table gives the {220} lattice spacing as d₂₂₀ = 1.920 155 716(32) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with the same relative uncertainty.\n\nThe table defines this quantity as the unit-cell edge length of an ideal, impurity-free, nearly perfect single crystal of natural silicon. Silicon is a cubic crystal with n = 8 atoms per facecentered cubic unit cell, so d₂₂₀ is the lattice parameter divided by √8, which the CODATA 2010 report also describes.\n\nEarlier adjustments give different numbers. The 2002 table lists d₂₂₀ as 192.015 5965(70) × 10⁻¹² m, and the 1998 table lists 192.015 5845(56) × 10⁻¹² m ({220} lattice spacing of Sia/ √8 d220 192.015 5965(70)× 10−12 m 3.6× 10−8 and {220} lattice spacing of Sia/ √8 d220 192.015 5845(56)× 10−12 m 2.9× 10−8). The search did not return the 2022 CODATA lattice parameter value, so I can't confirm whether it has changed since 2018."],"durationSeconds":5.500102451999992,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TbxM9WZEFgTH1dL2sNNX3z","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01REAU7wcE9JFFqWcCYgZsjA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Haas Tran Blaha 2009 PRB 79 085104 lattice constants PBE Si 5.475 Cu Al C Ge table\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals\",\"url\":\"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_constant\"},{\"title\":\"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals\"},{\"title\":\"nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3331871\"},{\"title\":\"suppl mater NJP108118 R1\",\"url\":\"https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_2599505_6/component/file_2618826/suppl-mater-NJP108118-R1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"assessing the performance of recent density functionals for 45szrev607\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/assessing-the-performance-of-recent-density-functionals-for-45szrev607.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing\"},{\"title\":\"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9330131/table/tbl1\"},{\"title\":\"peter blaha publication by citations\",\"url\":\"https://img.exaly.com/author-pdf/406567/peter-blaha-publication-by-citations.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search found the paper itself and some of its qualitative findings, but not the specific table values you asked for (PBE lattice constants for Si, Cu, Al, C, and Ge).\n\n- **Paper confirmed:** Haas, Tran, and Blaha, \"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals,\" PRB 79, 085104 (2009), received in November 2008 and published February 2009 ([source](https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf); [DOI record](https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals)). The study assessed LDA, several GGAs including PBE, and TPSS on a set of 60 cubic solids, with experimental values corrected for zero-point anharmonic expansion.\n- **Relevant trends:** A secondary summary says that for Al, most functionals except PBE give accurate lattice constants, and that for group IVA elements the trends follow nuclear charge ([source](https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529)). Those are the closest hits to your Al and Ge/C/Si entries, but they are qualitative, not numbers.\n- **Not verified:** I could not confirm the Si value of 5.475 Å or any Cu, C, or Ge PBE numbers from these results. You will need the full text or its supplementary tables to check them.\n- **Possible mismatch:** One result is PRB 79, 155107 (2009), a different Tran et al. paper on recent density functionals, not the 085104 paper ([source](https://typeset.io/pdf/assessing-the-performance-of-recent-density-functionals-for-45szrev607.pdf)).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ada1e56-dfe9-49b0-8def-90a47f44fa87","uuid":"dd521d5d-b781-4a1c-96f9-0ad72b5b0265","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:48:49.272Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Haas Tran Blaha 2009 PRB 79 085104 lattice constants PBE Si 5.475 Cu Al C Ge table","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014FcbTpenZn1WCnKaVFqZr1","content":[{"title":"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z","url":"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-