{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb41bNYQYd2MKhfUGLfT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: the equilibrium lattice constant of diamond-cubic silicon is a = 5.456 ± 0.03 Å.** It comes from MACE-MP-0 (medium, float64) with a Birch–Murnaghan equation-of-state fit, and is a static, 0 K value. That is 0.025 Å (+0.46%) above the measured CODATA value of 5.431020511 Å at 22.5 °C.\n\n**Premise check:** the question is well-posed. The 0 K lattice constant of a perfect crystal is a ground-state property, and a single-point energy-vs-volume scan gives it directly; no dynamics or timescale is involved. Cell size doesn't matter: the 8-atom cell and a 64-atom 2×2×2 supercell give the same answer to 10⁻⁵ Å. At the fitted size, forces are zero by symmetry and the leftover stress is 0.02 GPa.\n\n**Robustness of the fit:** I tried three equation-of-state forms (Birch–Murnaghan, Vinet, Murnaghan) over three strain ranges (±2, 4 and 6%). All nine gave a between 5.4556 and 5.4569 Å, so the fit itself is good to about ±0.001 Å. The fitted bulk modulus is 74.5 GPa.\n\n**Validation:** the calibration file at `/data/calibration/tier1.json` has no lattice constants, so I ran the same model and protocol on four other solids. I compared them with the room-temperature values in Wikipedia's \"Lattice constant\" table:\n\n| | MACE (Å) | Experiment (Å) | Deviation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C (diamond) | 3.571 | 3.567 | +0.1% |\n| Al | 4.060 | 4.046 | +0.3% |\n| **Si** | **5.456** | **5.4310** | **+0.46%** |\n| Cu | 3.626 | 3.597 | +0.8% |\n| Ge | 5.773 | 5.658 | +2.0% |\n\nEvery value comes out slightly too large. That fits the model having been trained on PBE-level DFT data, which tends to overestimate lattice constants. The Wikipedia Cu value (3.597 Å) is lower than other commonly quoted figures, so that row is only a rough check.\n\n**Uncertainty (±0.03 Å):** this is the model's systematic error, sized from the gaps to experiment for Si and the other solids above. The fitting error is negligible beside it.\n\n**Limitations (recorded in the note, not as flags):**\n- Zero-point motion and thermal expansion are not included, while the measured value includes them. For Si that correction should be small compared with the model error, but I did not calculate it.\n- The number is essentially a PBE-level prediction, not an independent prediction of experiment.\n- I tried to retrieve published PBE lattice constants to check the model against its DFT parent directly. The Materials Project page returned a 403 error and the paper's PDF came back empty, so I didn't quote any PBE numbers.\n- I ran MACE through ASE rather than the ALCHEMI dynamics pipeline, since a static scan needs no time-stepping.\n\n**On the board:**\n- Candidate `4e9460a5-f9e3-4589-bc43-fd28aefbd0ba`, which fulfils need #0.\n- Property result `309dcc22-361b-4448-a2e5-3fbd2efecbe4`, with no flags.\n\nMy first property_result post was rejected because it gave a simulation length of 0 ps. The repost uses null instead, so only one result is on the board. No open needs remain.\n\nFiles are in /work:\n- eos.py (in scripts/)\n- eos_results.json\n- result.json\n\nSources:\n- [NIST CODATA 2018 X-ray values (Si lattice parameter)](https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf)\n- [Wikipedia: Lattice constant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_constant)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":37053,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ada1e56-dfe9-49b0-8def-90a47f44fa87","uuid":"7f55f760-ffa6-4ac9-8025-c6b6027adec0","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:49:46.651Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb41azUVnw2u97nndhkj"}
